  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nEarly this morning, upper air analysis shows a rather nebulous \npattern across the central CONUS. A very weak and subtle trough is \nslowly trekking across the central plains, only bringing enough\nlift for a few high clouds across the region. Meanwhile, at the\nsurface, a weak surface ridge remains placed over the Missouri \nRiver Valley supporting light winds across the forecast area. \nIn areas where there's less coverage of high clouds, dense fog \nis beginning to develop, especially in creek and river valleys \nacross central Kansas. In addition, ample radiational cooling \nhas allowed for temperatures to fall into the upper 20s to low \n30s along and west of the Flint Hills. Meanwhile, low stratus \nremains quite stubborn across southeast Kansas which is helping \nto keep temperatures in the 40s this morning. With upper level \ncloud cover exiting eastward, diurnal heating should be \nsufficient enough to mix out fog and low stratus throughout the \nday and allow for afternoon highs to rise into the 50s and 60s. \nWeak surface winds and mostly clear skies tonight should allow \nfor temperatures tonight to dip into the 30s.\n\nModest mid/upper ridging and weak southerly flow at the surface will \nsupport a gradual warm up heading during the latter half of the \nweekend and into the beginning of next week. Afternoon highs Sunday \nthrough Tuesday will rise into the 60s and low 70s, and overnight \nlows will generally bottom out into the 40s. A very weak frontal \nboundary is currently forecast to pass through the region late \nMonday or early Tuesday, then another appears likely early on\nWednesday. However, these frontal passages this will do little to \nimpact temperatures until late next week. Lastly, precipitation \nappears unlikely over the next 7 days.",
